---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site, pillars } from "../data/site.js";
const strategy = pillars.find((p) => p.slug === "tax-strategy");

const title = "Tax Strategy for Business Owners | Advisant Financial";
const description =
  "Entity structure, owner compensation, retirement plan design, depreciation, multi-state exposure, and exit planning for business owners in Denver and Metro Detroit.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  "@id": `${site.domain}/tax-strategy/#service`,
  name: "Tax Strategy for Business Owners",
  description,
  provider: { "@id": `${site.domain}/#organization` },
  areaServed: [
    { "@type": "State", name: "Colorado" },
    { "@type": "State", name: "Michigan" },
  ],
};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/tax-strategy/" schema={schema}>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="formline formline--onDark">Business owners</p>
          <h1>The decisions that move your tax bill are structural.</h1>
          <p>
            Deductions are arithmetic. Structure is strategy. Whether the entity is right,
            whether the salary is defensible, whether the retirement plan is sized to what the
            business can actually absorb — those set the number, and they are all decided
            before a return exists.
          </p>
          <div class="hero__actions">
            <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="#exit">Exit planning</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <aside class="credential">
          <div class="credential__seal" aria-hidden="true">EA</div>
          <h2>Year-round, not seasonal.</h2>
          <p>
            Most of what we do has a deadline that precedes the filing deadline, sometimes by
            months.
          </p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Cadence</dt><dd>Quarterly, plus as needed</dd>
            <dt>Credential</dt><dd>Enrolled Agent (IRS)</dd>
            <dt>Representation</dt><dd>All 50 states</dd>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="formline">Structure</p>
        <h2>Entity, compensation, and the things that compound</h2>
        <p>
          Owners tend to inherit their structure rather than choose it. An LLC formed in a
          hurry, an S election made on a preparer's suggestion five years ago, a salary set by
          what felt reasonable at the time. Each was defensible when made. Whether they are
          still right is a different question, and nobody asks it during filing season.
        </p>

        <h3>Entity selection and conversion</h3>
        <p>
          The S election saves self-employment tax up to a point and creates cost and
          constraint past it. There is a profit level below which it does not pay for itself
          and an ownership complexity above which it becomes a liability. Conversions run both
          directions and both have consequences — built-in gains, accumulated adjustments,
          basis — that take years to work through.
        </p>

        <h3>Reasonable compensation</h3>
        <p>
          The most examined number on an S corporation return. Set it too low and the exam
          risk is real; set it too high and you are paying payroll tax on distributions.
          Defensible means documented at the time with comparable data, not reconstructed
          afterward.
        </p>

        <h3>Retirement plan design</h3>
        <p>
          The largest legitimate deduction available to most profitable owners, and the most
          underused. A solo 401(k) with profit sharing, a cash balance plan layered on top, or
          a defined benefit plan for an owner with few employees and a short runway to
          retirement can absorb far more than owners assume. Plan design has to match the
          census, and the establishment deadlines are not the same as the funding deadlines.
        </p>

        <h3>Pass-through entity elections</h3>
        <p>
          Both Colorado and Michigan allow entity-level tax that bypasses the federal SALT
          cap. Neither is automatic, both have timing rules, and Michigan's is irrevocable for
          three years. We model per owner — it is common for an election to help three owners
          and be neutral for a fourth.
        </p>

        <h3>Timing and method</h3>
        <p>
          Accounting method, inventory treatment, depreciation elections, and the choice
          between bonus depreciation and Section 179 all move income between years. On owned
          real estate, cost segregation can accelerate substantial deductions — worth modeling
          against recapture on eventual sale rather than taken reflexively.
        </p>

        <h3>Qualified business income</h3>
        <p>
          The deduction phases against wage and property limitations, which means wage policy,
          entity structure, and asset ownership all interact with it. Owners near a threshold
          often have more control over the outcome than they realize.
        </p>

        <h3>Multi-state exposure</h3>
        <p>
          Hiring one person in another state can create registration, withholding,
          unemployment insurance, and income tax filing obligations. It is cheap to handle
          before it happens and expensive afterward, because the notices arrive with penalties
          attached.
        </p>

        <h2 id="exit">Exit planning: the tax on your sale is decided years earlier</h2>
        <p>
          By the time a letter of intent is signed, most of the structure is fixed. Owners who
          keep the most started two or three years out. The difference between a well-structured
          exit and a poorly structured one on the same purchase price is routinely seven figures
          on a mid-sized deal — and most of it is ordinary decisions made early enough to still
          be available.
        </p>

        <h3>Asset versus stock</h3>
        <p>
          Buyers want assets for the stepped-up basis. Sellers usually want stock for capital
          treatment and a cleaner liability break. For S corporations, a §338(h)(10) or §336(e)
          election gives the buyer asset treatment while preserving a single level of tax for
          the seller. That is a trade to price, not a term to accept.
        </p>

        <h3>Purchase price allocation</h3>
        <p>
          In an asset deal, how the price is allocated across classes determines whether
          proceeds are capital or ordinary. Goodwill, equipment subject to depreciation
          recapture, inventory, and a personal goodwill argument all land differently. Both
          parties file a single consistent schedule, so it is negotiated once.
        </p>

        <h3>C corporation double tax</h3>
        <p>
          A C corporation asset sale is taxed at the entity and again on distribution. If
          converting to S status is on the table, the built-in gains period means the clock
          starts years before the sale. This is the most common structural regret we see.
        </p>

        <h3>Installment sales, earnouts, and residency</h3>
        <p>
          Spreading gain can lower the effective rate and manage the net investment income tax,
          but it does not apply to recapture and it puts you in a creditor position on your own
          sale. Separately, where you are resident at the moment of sale can move the state tax
          to zero or fail to — and a residency change made carelessly during a deal invites an
          audit rather than avoiding tax.
        </p>

        <h3>Charitable timing</h3>
        <p>
          Contributing appreciated stock to a charitable remainder trust or donor advised fund
          before a binding agreement exists can remove gain from the sale entirely while
          producing a deduction. Once the sale is effectively negotiated, the assignment-of-income
          doctrine applies and the window is gone.
        </p>

        <h2>What this covers</h2>
        <ul>{strategy.points.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>
      </div>

      <aside class="facts">
        <p class="formline">Worth a conversation if</p>
        <ul class="ticklist">
          <li>Profit has outgrown the current structure</li>
          <li>You are hiring in a second or third state</li>
          <li>You are bringing on or buying out a partner</li>
          <li>You operate as a C corporation</li>
          <li>A sale is plausible within five years</li>
          <li>A buyer has approached you unsolicited</li>
          <li>You own the building your business occupies</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4rem;font-size:.9rem">
          <a href="/fit-check/">Advisor or preparer? Take the fit check &rarr;</a>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
